    {
      "q": "What if my child gets frustrated and gives up on a spot-the-difference puzzle?",
      "a": "That's totally normal! Start with puzzles that have fewer differences (like 3-5) and let your child take breaks. Celebrate every find, and never correct them—let them discover missed differences later or on a new try. The goal is enjoyment, not perfection."
    },
    {
      "q": "How often should I use spot-the-difference activities with my reluctant learner?",
      "a": "Let your child lead. Some kids want to do one puzzle daily for a week, others prefer once a week. The key is to stop while they're still having fun—that way they'll want to come back. Never force it."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can spot-the-difference puzzles really help with reading or math skills?",
      "a": "Absolutely! Visual discrimination—the ability to notice subtle differences—is a foundational skill for reading (distinguishing 'b' from 'd') and math (spotting patterns). Plus, the focus and patience built during puzzles transfer to academic tasks."
    },
    {
      "q": "My child is 4 years old. Are photorealisitic puzzles too advanced?",
      "a": "Not if you choose the right version! The Streets series offers varying difficulty. For a 4-year-old, just ask them to find one difference at a time, and point to it together. The photorealistic images are actually more engaging for young children because they look like real places they know."
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the difference between cartoony spot-the-difference puzzles and photorealistic ones?",
      "a": "Cartoony puzzles often have exaggerated differences (like color changes or missing objects). Photorealistic puzzles require closer observation because the differences are more subtle—like a missing brick or a shifted shadow. This builds deeper focus and real-world observation skills, which is why we recommend them for reluctant learners who need a genuine challenge."
